    "description": "The Order Service lets clients edit an order as one business unit. An Order owns its line items, shipment address, and payment allocations; removing a line must remove its persisted row, while adding a line and changing the shipping address must succeed or fail together. Clients never address lines or addresses directly, and the database exposes no endpoint that loads or saves them independently. The persistence layer must translate this object graph to the relational tables and coordinate the complete write, including deletions and optimistic version checks, without exposing the physical table layout to callers. A recent release persisted an address change while its new line failed, and support traced three similar partial writes to callers sequencing table operations by hand.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose the ownership boundary for persisting an order and all of its dependent parts as one synchronized unit of work.",
    "notes": "composite-entity still fits the order persisted as one business unit whose parts have no independent lifecycle, with dependent-mapping the parent-maps-children near miss; fixed by adding partial-write incident evidence to the description."
